# Your task

Produce a ValidatedScenario. Three things matter, in order.

## 1. Success criteria must be OBSERVABLE ON SCREEN

This is the rule that makes the whole system worth anything. The rig proves
things by looking at video frames from a capture card. If a criterion cannot be
seen in a frame, it cannot be checked, and a test that cannot fail is worthless.

Rewrite vague criteria into visual ones:

  BAD:  "the game launches"
  GOOD: "the game's main menu is visible on screen, showing its title"

  BAD:  "navigation works"
  GOOD: "the highlighted item in the dashboard row moves to a different tile"

  BAD:  "no errors"
  GOOD: "no error dialog or 'Something went wrong' message is visible"

Set `check_type` for each criterion:
  - `screen_change`    something on screen must visibly change
  - `text_present`     specific text must appear (put it in parameters.text)
  - `text_absent`      specific text must NOT appear
  - `screen_match`     the frame should match a known screen
  - `vision_judgement` a vision model judges the frame against a description
  - `no_error_dialog`  no error UI is present

Prefer `text_present` and `screen_change` where you can - they are the most
reliable. Use `vision_judgement` when the check genuinely needs interpretation.

## 2. Reference only controls that exist

If the request names a button, check it against the control list above. Put
control names in a criterion's `parameters.button` so they can be verified
before anything is executed. Never invent a control name.

## 3. Be honest about what is unclear

If the request is too vague to test - no way to tell success from failure - set
`valid: false` and explain what is missing in `issues`. A rejected scenario is
far more useful than a scenario that passes for the wrong reason.

Put anything ambiguous but not fatal into `warnings`, and state assumptions in
`preconditions` (e.g. "the console is on the dashboard, signed in").

# Things worth knowing about this rig

- Game load times vary enormously by title and install state. If the scenario
  involves launching a game, set a generous `timeout_seconds`.
- First runs can show unexpected dialogs: updates, EULAs, profile pickers. If
  the scenario cannot tolerate these, mention it in `preconditions`.
- Protected content (streaming apps) captures as a black frame due to HDCP.
  Any scenario involving one is unverifiable - reject it and say why.

# Output

Return a ValidatedScenario. Give it a short kebab-case `id`, a human-readable
`title`, and a `goal` written as a direct instruction to whoever executes it.
